WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.201 --> 00:00:01.846
- [Narrator] Principles for Success:

00:00:01.846 --> 00:00:04.084
an Ultra Mini-Series Adventure

00:00:04.084 --> 00:00:06.751
in 30 minutes and in 8 episodes.

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:10.690
Episode 6, Your Two Biggest Barriers.

00:00:12.731 --> 00:00:16.041
I can't tell you which path
in life is best for you

00:00:16.041 --> 00:00:18.007
because I don't know how important it is

00:00:18.007 --> 00:00:21.751
for you to achieve big goals
relative to how important it is

00:00:21.751 --> 00:00:25.703
for you to avoid the pains
required to get them.

00:00:25.703 --> 00:00:28.015
This is the courage I spoke of earlier

00:00:28.015 --> 00:00:31.423
and we each have to feel these
things out for ourselves.

00:00:31.423 --> 00:00:34.145
After my big mistake in
calling for a depression,

00:00:34.145 --> 00:00:38.789
I had come to one of life's
forks in the road as we all do.

00:00:38.789 --> 00:00:42.264
If I made the choice to take
a normal job and play it safe,

00:00:42.264 --> 00:00:43.407
I would have ended up with

00:00:43.407 --> 00:00:47.518
a very different life than the one I had.

00:00:47.518 --> 00:00:50.735
But as long as I can pay the
rent, put food on the table,

00:00:50.735 --> 00:00:54.635
and educate my kids, the only
choice for me was to risk

00:00:54.635 --> 00:00:58.432
crossing the jungle in pursuit
of the best life possible.

00:00:58.432 --> 00:01:01.555
My big mistake in betting on a depression

00:01:01.555 --> 00:01:04.620
gave me a healthy fear of being wrong,

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.911
in other words, it gave me deep humility,

00:01:07.911 --> 00:01:10.307
which was exactly what I needed.

00:01:10.307 --> 00:01:12.107
At the same time, it didn't stop me

00:01:12.107 --> 00:01:15.423
from aggressively going
after the things I wanted.

00:01:15.423 --> 00:01:20.423
To succeed, I needed to see
more than I alone can see.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.745
But standing in my way of doing that

00:01:22.745 --> 00:01:26.762
were the two biggest
barriers everyone faces:

00:01:26.762 --> 00:01:30.161
our ego and blind spot barriers.

00:01:30.161 --> 00:01:33.388
These barriers exist because
of how our brains work.

00:01:33.388 --> 00:01:36.578
First, let's explore the ego barrier.

00:01:36.578 --> 00:01:38.620
When I refer to your ego barrier,

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.324
I'm talking about the parts
of your brain that prevent you

00:01:41.324 --> 00:01:44.152
from acknowledging your
weaknesses objectively

00:01:44.152 --> 00:01:47.086
so that you can figure
out how to deal with it.

00:01:47.086 --> 00:01:50.862
Your deepest-seated needs
and fears reside in areas

00:01:50.862 --> 00:01:53.962
of your brain that control
your emotions and are not

00:01:53.962 --> 00:01:58.148
accessible to your higher
level conscious awareness

00:01:58.148 --> 00:02:00.942
and because our need to be
right can be more important

00:02:00.942 --> 00:02:03.907
than our need to find out what's true,

00:02:03.907 --> 00:02:06.250
we like to believe our own opinions

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:08.937
without properly stress testing them.

00:02:08.937 --> 00:02:10.529
We especially don't like to look

00:02:10.529 --> 00:02:12.759
at our mistakes and weaknesses.

00:02:12.759 --> 00:02:15.267
We are instinctively prone to react

00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:18.959
to explorations of them
as though they're attacks.

00:02:18.959 --> 00:02:21.803
We get angry, even though
it would be more logical

00:02:21.803 --> 00:02:24.850
for us to be open to feedback from others.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.550
This leads to our making
inferior decisions,

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:31.355
learning less, and falling
short of our potentials.

00:02:31.355 --> 00:02:33.987
The second is the blind spot barrier.

00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:36.153
Everyone has blind spots.

00:02:36.153 --> 00:02:38.636
The blind spot barrier
is when a person believes

00:02:38.636 --> 00:02:42.097
he or she can see everything,
but it's a simple fact

00:02:42.097 --> 00:02:46.906
that no one alone can see a
complete picture of reality.

00:02:46.906 --> 00:02:50.248
Naturally people can't
appreciate what they can't see,

00:02:50.248 --> 00:02:53.353
just as we all have
different ranges for singing,

00:02:53.353 --> 00:02:55.774
hearing pitch, and seeing colors, we have

00:02:55.774 --> 00:02:59.667
different ranges for seeing
and understanding things.

00:02:59.667 --> 00:03:01.903
For example, while some people are better

00:03:01.903 --> 00:03:06.736
at seeing the big picture,
others excel at seeing details.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:11.890
Some are linear thinkers
and others are more lateral.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:14.907
While some are creative but not reliable,

00:03:14.907 --> 00:03:19.211
others are reliable but
not creative, and so on.

00:03:19.211 --> 00:03:21.732
Because of how our brains
are wired differently,

00:03:21.732 --> 00:03:25.811
everyone perceives the world
around them differently.

00:03:25.811 --> 00:03:28.109
By doing what comes naturally to us,

00:03:28.109 --> 00:03:32.499
we fail to account for our
weaknesses and we crash.

00:03:32.499 --> 00:03:35.651
Either we keep doing that or we change.

00:03:35.651 --> 00:03:39.099
Aristotle defined tragedy
as a terrible outcome

00:03:39.099 --> 00:03:41.832
arising from a person's fatal flaw.

00:03:41.832 --> 00:03:44.266
A flaw that, had it been fixed,

00:03:44.266 --> 00:03:48.004
would have instead led
to a wonderful outcome.

00:03:48.004 --> 00:03:51.487
In my opinion, these two
barriers are the main impediments

00:03:51.487 --> 00:03:54.738
that get in the way of
good decision-making.

00:03:54.738 --> 00:03:58.678
My fear of being wrong gave
me the radical open-mindedness

00:03:58.678 --> 00:04:01.761
I needed and that changed everything.

